Article 25
(1) The Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility to implement protection efforts for victims of violence in the form of:
   a. preventing the occurrence of all forms of gender-based and child violence;
   b. providing protection by providing complaint services, health services, social rehabilitation services, legal aid and law enforcement services, repatriation and social reintegration services, seeking to increase community participation, as well as monitoring and reporting;
   c. encouraging and increasing community participation; and
   d. conducting cooperation with service providers in efforts to prevent, protect, and recover victims of violence.
(2) To carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government has the authority to:
   a. formulate policies and programs on the elimination of gender-based and child violence;
   b. establish integrated service centers;
   c. establish complaint service institutions at the sub-district level;
   d. facilitate the implementation of integrated service centers and the activities of complaint service institutions at the sub-district level;
   e. provide facilities and infrastructure;
   f. increase the capacity of service provider institutions;
   g. conduct coordination and cooperation in the implementation of protection for victims of gender-based and child violence;
   h. encourage and increase community participation; and
   i. conduct monitoring and evaluation.
(3) In the implementation of its obligations, responsibilities, and authorities, the Regent may delegate to Regional Apparatus.
(4) Regional Apparatus receiving delegation from the Regent as referred to in paragraph (3) shall, in its implementation, prepare a Regional Action Plan for victim protection.
(5) The Regional Government, in carrying out the obligations as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), must pay attention to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, husbands, wives, or other persons who are legally responsible for the victim.

Article 26
The community, family, and parents have the obligation and responsibility to:
a. 	prevent the occurrence of violence against women and children;
b. 	report if violence occurs;
c. 	protect victims; and
d. 	provide emergency assistance.

Article 17
Increasing Community Participation as referred to in Article 9 letter h is carried out by means of: fostering community awareness of cases of gender-based violence and violence against children;
encouraging the community to actively participate in providing information and reporting the occurrence of gender-based violence and violence against children;
fostering local wisdom in handling gender-based violence and violence against children;
organizing the strengthening of community groups in handling gender-based violence and violence against children; and disseminating information about laws and regulations related to gender-based violence and violence against children.

CHAPTER VI
COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION
Article 27
(1) The community may participate in assisting efforts to prevent and handle victims of gender-based violence and child victims.
(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in the form of:
    a. providing information and/or reporting any violence that comes to their knowledge;
    b. providing protection for victims;
    c. providing emergency assistance;
    d. providing advocacy to victims and/or the community regarding the handling of gender-based violence and child victims;
    e. assisting in the process of submitting applications for the determination of protection; and
    f. assisting in the process of repatriation and social reintegration.
(3) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.

Article 16
Coordination and cooperation as referred to in Article 9 letter g includes: conducting coordination and cooperation in handling cases of violence with UPPTK;
conducting coordination and cooperation with integrated service centers between regencies/cities; and conducting coordination and cooperation with institutions providing services for victims of gender-based violence and children.

Article 22
Every victim of gender-based violence and children has the right: to obtain protection for personal security, family and property, and freedom from threats related to testimony that will be, is being, or has been given;
to participate in the process of selecting and determining the form of protection and security support;
freedom from ensnaring questions;
to obtain information regarding case developments and court decisions; and to obtain complaint services, health services, social rehabilitation services, legal aid and enforcement services, as well as repatriation and social reintegration services.
The rights of victims as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 20
UPPTK as referred to in Article 5 paragraph (1) letter b has the duties of: receiving complaints of gender-based and child violence cases;
providing initial consultation to victims of gender-based and child violence;
conducting crisis intervention for victims of gender-based and child violence; and providing referrals for handling gender-based and child violence cases to PPT PKBGA.

Article 3
The Objectives of the Implementation of Gender-Based and Child Victim Protection are to: prevent all forms of gender-based violence and violence against children, occurring within the scope of households and/or communities;
provide protection in the form of complaint services, health services, social rehabilitation services, legal aid and enforcement services, as well as repatriation and social reintegration services; and endeavor to increase community participation.

